    "description": "Linux Kernel development - Architected and developed solutions in Linux kernel.  Developed early Proof of Concept (POC) implementations for new technologies in the Linux kernel that enabled designers assess various aspects of the features e.g. performance, get early feedback from Linux upstream community, prepare other development activities and showcase the features to customers and business units. \n\nReal-time Linux - Researched the latency impact of CPU power saving features and delivered methodologies that allow power saving without impacting real-time performance. The work enabled business groups to take advantage of CPU power management for their real-time customers.\n\nZephyr Real-time OS - As an early developer, designed and implemented core components and worked on making the OS ready for public launch. Added Power Management, File System and Tickless Kernel support. The implementations were for multiple architectures e.g. ARM, x86 and ARC.  \nMy commits can be found here https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/commits?author=ramesh-thomas\n\nVirtualization and Security - Worked on the Type-1 Hypervisor used in McAfee Deep Defender anti-virus software. Did extensive performance tuning to meet the speed requirements. Optimized VMEXIT/VMENTERs and Extended Page Table (EPT) management. Obtained 3 patents for this work.  Restructured the hypervisor making it lighter for use with Intel Kernel Guard Technology.\n\nGraphics Kernel Driver - Wrote kernel-mode Windows drivers for Intel GPU.  Designed and implemented kernel hardware abstraction layers that enabled efficient reuse of the driver. Ported assembly to C, improving maintainability and scalability. Worked on system and device power management. Worked extensively on platform bring up.",
